Subject: YANI: Kid Gloves
[sop for those who treasure broken news software]

Sometimes, like when playing a pacifist, or when
trying to work the protection racket still at level
1, you _really_, _really_, don't want to do harm to
a single monster, but back in the real world an
offspring pops for attention with a demand for a
peanut butter sandwich, your eyes move but your
fingers don't stop, and you've managed to murder
some pitiful defenseless monsters with your bare
hands, losing a conduct or doubling a bribe.

Wouldn't it be nice if you could somehow cover your
hands in some material that really cut down your
chances of doing harm when you're trying to sneak
past sleeping monsters interspersed with gas spores
or similar riff-raff, or hurrying down a hall to
make it to a shop before closing time, or the baby
in your (real world) lap just jammed her pacifier
into the keyboard keys, or one or the other "you"
are otherwise slightly distracted?

"Padded gloves" are already in use, but "kid
gloves", "velvet gloves", and "boxing gloves" (whose
purpose, after all, is to decrease murderous blows
to merely incapacitating ones) are all available as
alternatives, with the Dev Team to decide just how
big a fraction of collisions should be harmless
without causing total imbalance.  Probably there
should be associated AC penalties for wearing these
foppish things. You could also, for folks _not_
trying to be feather-fisted, like monks, consider
these to be some more of the _un_helpful dungeon
objects liberally scattered about the dungeon.

This is really a desire of my current healer, about
to enter mines toplevel with zero XP, AC 0,
invisible, rich and gaining wealth quickly, and
accompanied by her large LVL 8 cat Purrball I(*),
from level 3, the second level with a cross-aligned
(**) altar so far.

The reason, of course, is the Hippocratic Oath to
which all healers are sworn: "First, do no harm." This
is quite hard for her, given the impatience, rapid
typing speed, and limited attention span of her
player-partner super-ego.

[She ran a _lot_ of circles on level three trying
to find her no-good lay-about cat, with jackels and
grid bugs in hot pursuit, and lichens bringing up
the rear. A hot soaking bath would be nice about
now.]

xanthian.

(*) It inspires quite a bit of HP-envy that even
though my PC has quaffed all her healing potions at
game start in a standard strategy (so the points
added get used over and over for the duration of the
game rather than burned out in a single episode),
her large cat has 58 HP and she has merely 24.

Still, it is the cat's good fortune, since, wearing
Dwarvish Mithral just found lying about on level 2,
the healer isn't about to be successful casting
"healing" at the cat any time soon.

(**) Hermes has really got his work cut out for
him. So far, Athena seems to own the joint.

Hermes would probably get a lot more help stamping
out the heresies of Athena worship from this healer
if His historical record of gratitude were more
abundant, evident, and long lasting, when a dangerous
"altar conversion" evocation had been successfully
accomplished after a large monster chase.

Frankly, you get the impression He really doesn't
notice.

Would a few "attaboys", "attagirls", or "artifacts"
be so far out of order? ;-)

The DoD needs some new "mini-artifacts" of powers
less than a named blade or quest artifact, but more
impressive (even if less useful) than a spellbook.

Maybe some bauble that sends monsters who see it
into hysterical laughter for a turn or two, or other
ideas along the same lines?

"Wobbly Walk" brand stiletto heel lady's pumps, to
up kicking damage and do special damage to icky
nasty oozy floor thingies, and all rats, would be a
fine accompanyment to the chain mail bikini.

For the pimply-faced adventurers misled by their
governments into volunteer DoD service, lead-soled
platform clogs, more effective against bugs than
even boots of kicking, might work better, those kids
are mostly unbalanced anyway and could use some
ballasting.

Zoot Suit style formal wear armor with pocket watch
hunger forecaster on a chain would be a "retro"
touch for the grizzled-adventurer types, as would
tasteful boom boxes playing Perry Como tunes to lull
monsters to sleep.

A Dilbert-style engineer's +1 (max) magic pocket
protector, and similar silly stuff could surely also
be devised for the geeky types, to reward the smaller,
but still noteworthy, accomplishments of an adventurer,
while incapacitating intelligent sighted monsters with
short spells of convulsions.



